.TH TMV 1 "2026-11-25" "tmv 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmv \- process memory layout, allocation attribution and offline replay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmv
[\fIPID\fR]
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B tmv run
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \-\- \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B tmv attach
\fIPID\fR
.br
.B tmv open
\fISESSION.tmvr\fR [\-\-at \fISECONDS\fR]
.br
.B tmv report
\fISESSION.tmvr\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmv
reads the virtual memory map of a running Linux process from
.IR /proc/\fIPID\fR
and renders the address space as a
.BR WizTree -style
treemap: every rectangle is a mapping, and its area is proportional to the
selected metric (virtual size, RSS, PSS, private, shared or swap).
.PP
Sampling is read-only and requires either the same user ID as the target or
.BR CAP_SYS_PTRACE .
Without \fBsmaps\fR permissions the viewer falls back to virtual sizes.
.PP
The interface is a full-screen TUI; press \fB?\fR for the key reference and
\fBq\fR to quit. Memory contents can be inspected with \fBx\fR, which reads
from
.IR /proc/\fIPID\fR/mem .
.PP
The original layout viewer remains the default. The allocation viewer tracks
outstanding requested bytes and allocation call stacks; these bytes are not
RSS
and must not be added to RSS. Business labels require build-id-bound naming
rules,
not automatic inference of C/C++ structure types.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "tmv attach " PID
Open the original live layout viewer. Does not inject a collector or
reconstruct
past allocations. Equivalent to \fBtmv PID\fR.
.TP
.BI "tmv run " "[OPTIONS] -- PROGRAM [ARGS...]"
Start a same-architecture dynamically linked Linux/glibc program with the
bundled
LD_PRELOAD collector. Record periodic layout and allocation snapshots. The
default
sampling interval is one second. Quitting the viewer leaves both the target
and
the recorder running; the recorder completes after the target exits normally.
.TP
.BI "tmv open " "SESSION [--at SECONDS]"
Browse a .tmvr directory. Follow while recording; when finished, enable
offline
replay and the time chart. \fB--at\fR requires a finished session and selects
the
last sample at or before elapsed SECONDS since recording started, not
wall-clock
time. A time before the first sample is an error. Without --at, the viewer
opens
the last sample containing layout data when available.
For unresolved allocation frames, open and report use the recorded mapping and
GNU build ID to locate a matching ELF at its recorded path or at
SESSION/symbols/BUILD_ID. Supply an unstripped ELF there for offline replay.
Default groups use the module and calling function, falling back to the
original allocation stack.
C++ overload signatures are preserved; individual frames retain offsets.
.TP
.BI "tmv report " "SESSION [OPTIONS]"
Print statistics from a recorded sample, defaulting to the last sample. The
final
sample may have no process layout after exit; select an earlier --at for
layout.
.SH "RUN OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-output " DIRECTORY"
Create a new session directory; it must not exist. Default:
tmv-TIMESTAMP.tmvr.
.TP
.BI \-\-library " FILE"
Collector library. Search order: explicit option, TMV_TRACE_LIB, library
beside the
executable, /usr/lib64/tmv/libtmvtrace.so, development source directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-rules " FILE"
JSON array of naming rules. Each rule requires label, build_id, function;
optional
return_offset is the decimal return-address offset within the function. Omit
the
offset to match allocations throughout the function. Conflicting rules fail.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SECONDS"
Sample every 1 to 3600 seconds; default 1. This is distinct from the top-level
live layout --interval, where 0 disables automatic refresh.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ui
Do not open a TUI. Preserve the target's standard input/output. Interactive
run
redirects target stdout/stderr to program.log; use --no-ui for interactive
targets.
.SH "REPORT OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-at " SECONDS"
Select the nearest preceding recorded sample relative to recording start.
.TP
.BI \-\-analysis " allocation|layout"
Default allocation: group outstanding requested bytes by label/call site.
Layout: group the saved process mappings by category using RSS.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Limit text rankings and appended stack groups; default 20.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output the entire selected frame, including layout and allocations with
stacks;
this takes precedence over text and SVG options, independently of --analysis.
.TP
.BI \-\-svg " FILE"
Export a Treemap SVG for the selected analysis. Session report does not
provide
--svg-format; the legacy layout/trace export supports treemap and flame.
.TP
.B \-\-stacks
Append allocation stacks to non-JSON output. Stack paths are grouped with byte
and block counts. Old recordings without captured stacks cannot reconstruct
them.
.SH "LAYOUT AND RAW TRACE OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \fIPID\fR
Target process ID. Optional for demo, snapshot replay, offline raw traces or
subcommands.
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Use synthetic data so the interface can be previewed without a Linux process.
.TP
.BI \-\-replay " FILE"
Replay a snapshot written by \fB\-\-json\fR or \fB\-\-save\-baseline\fR.
No sampling or process attachment takes place.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SECONDS"
Auto-refresh interval; \fB0\fR (the default) refreshes only when
.B r
is pressed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the snapshot as JSON and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Print a text report and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Number of entries in the report and diff lists (default \fB10\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " FILE"
Load a snapshot used as the comparison baseline.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-baseline " FILE"
Write the current snapshot to
.I FILE
as JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-metric " METRIC"
Metric used for areas and diffs: \fBvirtual\fR, \fBrss\fR, \fBpss\fR,
\fBprivate\fR, \fBshared\fR or \fBswap\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-lookup " ADDR"
Resolve a hexadecimal address to module, symbol and source line, then exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-read " ADDR[:LEN]"
Read memory at a hexadecimal address and print it in
.BR xxd (1)
style, then exit. \fILEN\fR defaults to 256 bytes and is capped at 65536.
.TP
.B \-\-stack
Unwind the main thread and print symbolized frames, then exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-svg " FILE"
Render the address space to a self-contained SVG file and exit.  Every block
carries a hover tooltip (path, size, address range, permissions); with the
treemap layout, clicking a group zooms into it (inline JavaScript, no external
assets).
.TP
.BI \-\-svg\-format " FORMAT"
Layout used by \fB\-\-svg\fR: \fBtreemap\fR (default, drill-down) or
\fBflame\fR (horizontal icicle graph).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not read or write the configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-trace " FILE"
Read a raw allocator .bin trace; default output is text rankings. With PID and
--interval, refresh while the target runs. Without PID, process it offline.
Use run/open for the interactive allocation TUI.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-maps " FILE"
Override captured maps; default is TRACE with the .maps extension.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-rules " FILE"
Apply build-id-bound business labels to raw trace statistics. Missing or
mismatched
ELF build-id falls back to addresses; keep binaries/debug files at recorded
paths.
.SH "LAYOUT KEYS"
Toolbar buttons show their keyboard shortcuts. In layout view, press 1, 2 or 3
to select the treemap, address map or mapping list.
In mapping and allocation lists, press uppercase U or click the unit control
to cycle through automatic units, bytes, KiB, MiB and GiB.
.TP
.B ?
Open the layout action menu. Click an action or press any key to dismiss.
.TP
.B arrows, hjkl
Move the selection; in list view move row by row.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Esc
Descend into the selected block / go back one level.
.TP
.B v, b
Toggle Treemap/list, or the basic address-ordered Blockmap. Each block
interval
is independently scaled with holes folded; it is not a uniformly scaled page
map.
.TP
.B f
Filter mappings by path, label or permissions (substring).
.TP
.B F
Filter mappings with a case-insensitive regular expression.
.TP
.B /
Resolve a hexadecimal address and jump to it.
.TP
.BR m ", " g ", " o
Cycle metric, grouping and sort order.
.TP
.BR p ", " t ", " a
Toggle page map, thread stacks and accounting panel.
.TP
.B x
Hex-dump the mapping under the cursor.
.TP
.B s
Open the settings panel.
.TP
.B r
Refresh once; press twice quickly to toggle auto-refresh.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B [, ], u
In the live thread panel: previous thread, next thread, refresh unwind.
.TP
.B d
Toggle changes relative to the previous snapshot/baseline.
.SH "MEMORY WINDOW"
Use x on a mapping or click a sampled page. Read 512 bytes at a time; arrows
and
PageUp/PageDown scroll, n/N read next/previous window, r rereads, i toggles
little-endian u64/f64 interpretation, c saves a comparison baseline. Changed
lines
are highlighted; changing address clears the baseline. Reads do not stop the
process and are not an atomic object snapshot. Historical memory bytes are not
recorded and cannot be read from an offline session.
.SH "SESSION KEYS"
.TP
.B ?
Show shortcuts for the current session view. Esc closes help.
.TP
.B Tab
Switch saved process layout / allocation attribution. Allocation selection
updates
the right-hand stack panel (below the chart on narrow terminals).
.TP
.B Enter, Backspace, arrows, v, b
Descend, return, select blocks, toggle list, toggle basic address Blockmap.
.TP
.B t, PageUp, PageDown
In allocation view: expand stack popup, scroll allocation stacks. Realloc
preserves
original allocation stack and separately records its latest successful resize.
.TP
.B [, ], End, Space
Previous/next sample, latest sample/resume following, pause/resume following
or
offline playback. These keys refer to samples, not threads, in session mode.
.TP
.B T, M, P
Offline only: enter elapsed seconds (such as 9.5), switch tracked
allocation/RSS
curve, jump to the sampled peak of the selected curve. The input shows the
current
time and valid range. Live sessions have no time curve; reopen after recording
ends.
.SH MOUSE
Left click selects, left drag sweeps selections, double click descends into a
group,
right click returns or closes a popup. Toolbar/menu/settings buttons are
clickable.
The wheel scrolls lists, details, stacks, accounting and memory contents. The
live
thread panel offers previous/next/refresh buttons; memory offers window,
numeric
and baseline buttons. Click or drag the address bar to navigate mappings.
.PP
In offline sessions, click or drag the time curve left/right to select the
nearest
preceding sample. Selection synchronizes layout, allocation blocks and stacks.
Dragging beyond the endpoints clamps to the first/last sample. Time curves
remain
offline-only. Terminal text selection may require the terminal's Shift
modifier.
.SH "RECORDING AND LIMITATIONS"
A .tmvr session is a directory with manifest.json, sampled frames/, raw trace,
module metadata, collector.log and (for interactive run) program.log. Copy the
entire directory. Samples capture layout and allocations sequentially, not
atomically.
Raw events currently have no timestamps; replay is sampled, not event-exact.
.PP
Curves hold the previous sample between measurements. Dense time buckets
preserve
sampled maxima; missing RSS is a gap, not zero. Short-lived peaks between
samples
are not represented. Cache in the bundled demo is freed around 12 seconds;
inspect
--at 9 to see Cache 32 MiB and Session.buffer 24 MiB together.
.PP
Allocator hooks cover malloc/calloc/realloc/free and supported aligned
allocators.
They do not yet attribute mmap/madvise events; use layout/RSS for mappings.
Static executables, allocator-internal/custom pool operations and
secure-execution
programs are not generally covered by LD_PRELOAD. Fork children do not append
to
the parent's trace. Module unload/address reuse is not reliably attributable.
.PP
Collection serializes writes and captures stacks, and may substantially affect
allocation-intensive programs. Sessions store all live allocations at each
sample;
there are no quotas or low-overhead sampling buffers. Use bounded diagnostic
runs.
Unknown frees and incomplete/error states must be considered when interpreting
data.
A complete recording does not imply all allocator interfaces were covered.
.PP
Layout provides VMA/category/file rankings, page residency/THP/shared/swap
sampling,
smaps_rollup accounting, KSM, cgroup v1/v2, NUMA and snapshot differences.
Availability
is subject to kernel support and permissions. ELF/DWARF and debug files
resolve
symbols; thread unwind uses CFI with frame-pointer fallback and briefly
attaches.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMV_TRACE_LIB
Explicit collector library used by run unless --library was supplied.
.TP
.B TMV_STACK_DEPTH
Allocator unwind depth, default 16, maximum 32; 0 disables captured stacks.
Collector/internal frames may reduce the number of displayed business frames.
.TP
.B TMV_TRACE
Raw trace filename prefix when launching LD_PRELOAD manually; output adds PID
and
\&.bin/.maps/.ids extensions. Existing event files are not overwritten.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/tmv
Bash Tab completion installed by the RPM. The bash-completion loader discovers
it automatically. It can also be sourced directly in an interactive Bash.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tmv/config.toml
Interface preferences written on exit; override the location with
.BR TMV_CONFIG .
.TP
.I /proc/PID/maps, /proc/PID/smaps
Address space and per-mapping accounting.
.TP
.I /proc/PID/mem
Memory contents, used by \fBx\fR and \fB\-\-read\fR.
.TP
.I /proc/PID/pagemap
Page residency subject to ptrace access checks; PFN-based sharing/THP
information
requires appropriate privilege (typically CAP_SYS_ADMIN/root).
.TP
.I /usr/lib64/tmv/libtmvtrace.so
Private collector installed by the openEuler RPM; never configured as a global
preload.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The following codes describe explicit exits from top-level layout commands.
Subcommand/raw-trace errors returned by main use status 1; argument parser
failures
use status 2.
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line argument.
.TP
.B 2
Process not found, or address without a symbol.
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient permissions.
.TP
.B 4
Other collection error, unsupported platform, or invalid snapshot file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B tmv 4242
Inspect process 4242 interactively.
.TP
.B tmv 4242 \-\-report \-\-top 20
Print the twenty largest mappings.
.TP
.B tmv 4242 \-\-read 0x7f0000000000:128
Hex-dump 128 bytes at a given address.
.TP
.B tmv 4242 \-\-svg mem.svg
Export the treemap as an interactive SVG file.
.TP
.B tmv 4242 \-\-svg flame.svg \-\-svg\-format flame
Export the address space as a flame graph.
.TP
.B tmv 4242 \-\-svg diff.svg \-\-baseline base.json
Highlight growth and shrinkage against a saved baseline.
.TP
.B tmv \-\-demo
Preview the interface on a non-Linux host.
.TP
.B tmv run \-\-output app.tmvr \-\-heap\-rules rules.json \-\- ./app
Record a program with business naming rules.
.TP
.B tmv run \-\-no\-ui \-\-output app.tmvr \-\- ./app
Record without taking over the terminal.
.TP
.B tmv open app.tmvr \-\-at 9
Inspect the sample at or before nine elapsed seconds.
.TP
.B tmv report app.tmvr \-\-at 9 \-\-stacks
Print allocation rankings and grouped call stacks.
.TP
.B tmv report app.tmvr \-\-at 9 \-\-analysis layout
Print saved RSS layout rankings.
.TP
.B tmv report app.tmvr \-\-at 9 \-\-json
Output the selected full sample as JSON.
.TP
.B tmv report app.tmvr \-\-at 9 \-\-svg heap.svg
Export the selected allocation Treemap.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
